    <node id="NCCN">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a nonprofit medical organization and alliance of cancer centers in the United States. It is widely known for publishing evidence-based cancer care guidelines for both clinicians and patients. Its NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology are paired with the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which translate expert-reviewed recommendations into easy-to-understand language. These patient guidelines help individuals understand their diagnosis, prepare for conversations with their care team, and take part in treatment decisions.

NCCN makes patient resources available through multiple channels, including the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer mobile app, the website NCCN.org/patientguidelines, and print or digital guides. The organization actively seeks patient input through online surveys and feedback pages, such as NCCN.org/patients/feedback, NCCN.org/patients/response, and NCCN.org/patients/comments. NCCN also offers a directory of NCCN Cancer Centers, helping patients locate specialized care and clinical trials across the United States.

The scope of NCCN's patient guidelines is broad, covering numerous cancer types and care topics. Specific titles include guidelines for mouth cancer, nasopharyngeal cancer, throat cancer, esophageal cancer, gastric and esophageal topics, gallbladder and bile duct tumors, liver cancer, pancreatic cancer (including pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors), colorectal cancer, colon cancer, rectal cancer, colorectal cancer screening, lung cancer (NSCLC and SCLC), breast cancer (including metastatic breast cancer), bladder cancer, kidney cancer, adrenal tumors, prostate cancer, bone cancer, soft tissue sarcoma, melanoma, squamous cell skin cancer, cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, lymphoma, primary CNS lymphoma, CLL/SLL, and ALL. In addition to disease-specific guidance, NCCN addresses supportive care and survivorship topics such as fertility preservation for adolescent and young adult cancer patients, chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, distress during cancer care, anemia and neutropenia, and immunotherapy side effects from immune checkpoint inhibitors. NCCN also publishes survivorship care guides and resources covering medical appointments, care teams, medical and family history, tests, treatment planning, and follow-up care, including recommendations for how often skin exams should be performed after treatment for squamous cell skin cancer.

NCCN's experts provide clinical recommendations not only for treatment but also for screening and follow-up. For example, they advise regular skin cancer screening for individuals with certain genetic syndromes or weakened immune systems, referral to specialized cancer centers, and cancer genetic counseling when appropriate. The organization also recommends that everyone with primary CNS lymphoma consider joining a clinical trial. Overall, NCCN serves as a comprehensive source of current, expert-reviewed cancer information, patient education, and clinical trial navigation, supporting both patients and providers in staying informed about best practices in cancer care.

Relationships:
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: COLORECTAL CANCER ALLIANCE
  - Description: The NCCN Guidelines for Patients list Colorectal Cancer Alliance as a resource.

*Note: The entity FIGHT COLORECTAL CANCER ("A colorectal cancer advocacy organization listed as a resource, with website fightcolorectalcancer.org.") and its relationship to COLORECTAL CANCER ALLIANCE ("Both organizations are listed as resources and focus on colorectal cancer.") were also retrieved during the search but have been excluded from this record per your instruction.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">32</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: NCCN
Description: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a nonprofit medical organization and alliance of cancer centers in the United States. It is widely known for publishing evidence-based cancer care guidelines for both clinicians and patients. Its NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology are paired with the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which translate expert-reviewed recommendations into easy-to-understand language. These patient guidelines help individuals understand their diagnosis, prepare for conversations with their care team, and take part in treatment decisions.

NCCN makes patient resources available through multiple channels, including the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer mobile app, the website NCCN.org/patientguidelines, and print or digital guides. The organization actively seeks patient input through online surveys and feedback pages, such as NCCN.org/patients/feedback, NCCN.org/patients/response, and NCCN.org/patients/comments. NCCN also offers a directory of NCCN Cancer Centers, helping patients locate specialized care and clinical trials across the United States.

The scope of NCCN's patient guidelines is broad, covering numerous cancer types and care topics. Specific titles include guidelines for mouth cancer, nasopharyngeal cancer, throat cancer, esophageal cancer, gastric and esophageal topics, gallbladder and bile duct tumors, liver cancer, pancreatic cancer (including pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors), colorectal cancer, colon cancer, rectal cancer, colorectal cancer screening, lung cancer (NSCLC and SCLC), breast cancer (including metastatic breast cancer), bladder cancer, kidney cancer, adrenal tumors, prostate cancer, bone cancer, soft tissue sarcoma, melanoma, squamous cell skin cancer, cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, lymphoma, primary CNS lymphoma, CLL/SLL, and ALL. In addition to disease-specific guidance, NCCN addresses supportive care and survivorship topics such as fertility preservation for adolescent and young adult cancer patients, chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, distress during cancer care, anemia and neutropenia, and immunotherapy side effects from immune checkpoint inhibitors. NCCN also publishes survivorship care guides and resources covering medical appointments, care teams, medical and family history, tests, treatment planning, and follow-up care, including recommendations for how often skin exams should be performed after treatment for squamous cell skin cancer.

NCCN's experts provide clinical recommendations not only for treatment but also for screening and follow-up. For example, they advise regular skin cancer screening for individuals with certain genetic syndromes or weakened immune systems, referral to specialized cancer centers, and cancer genetic counseling when appropriate. The organization also recommends that everyone with primary CNS lymphoma consider joining a clinical trial. Overall, NCCN serves as a comprehensive source of current, expert-reviewed cancer information, patient education, and clinical trial navigation, supporting both patients and providers in staying informed about best practices in cancer care.

Relationships:
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: COLORECTAL CANCER ALLIANCE
  - Description: The NCCN Guidelines for Patients list Colorectal Cancer Alliance as a resource.

*Note: The entity FIGHT COLORECTAL CANCER ("A colorectal cancer advocacy organization listed as a resource, with website fightcolorectalcancer.org.") and its relationship to COLORECTAL CANCER ALLIANCE ("Both organizations are listed as resources and focus on colorectal cancer.") were also retrieved during the search but have been excluded from this record per your instruction.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that uses medicines or drugs to kill cancer cells, primarily by damaging and destroying rapidly dividing cells throughout the body. It is most often a systemic therapy, meaning the drugs travel through the bloodstream to reach cells everywhere in the body, but it can also be delivered locally—for example, applied directly to the skin as a topical cream (such as 5-fluorouracil), delivered into the bladder (intravesically), or given by mouth, injection, or intravenous infusion. Chemotherapy is typically given in cycles of treatment days followed by rest days, and it may be used as a single agent or as a multi-agent regimen. It can be administered before surgery (neoadjuvant therapy) to shrink tumors, after surgery (adjuvant therapy) to eliminate remaining cancer cells, or as the primary treatment. When combined with radiation, it is called chemoradiation; it may also be given after chemoradiation to kill lingering cancer cells.

Chemotherapy is the standard-of-care systemic treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it kills fast-dividing cells and is often multi-agent and given in cycles. It is also a main treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, with methotrexate being the principal drug; it can be combined with targeted therapy, and high-dose chemotherapy may be part of a stem cell rescue. For cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) that has spread, chemotherapy may be used when surgery and radiation are not possible or unsuccessful. In colorectal cancer, chemotherapy is recommended after colectomy for all stage 3 and some stage 2 colon cancers, and it is useful for pMMR/MSS cancers when given first; rectal cancer regimens may include oxaliplatin or irinotecan. For ovarian cancer, chemotherapy may be given first when surgery is not initially possible (with preferred regimens including paclitaxel and carboplatin), or after surgery to address residual disease. For cervical cancer, chemotherapy is combined with radiation as chemoradiation, often given once weekly. For anal cancer, chemotherapy is used together with radiation, and also for cancer that returns to lymph nodes or becomes metastatic. For bladder cancer, chemotherapy is one of the main intravesical therapies (delivered directly into the bladder), and may also be given systemically before or after surgery.

Side effects of chemotherapy can include nausea, vomiting, fatigue, fever, infection, tingling or numbness, rash, and diarrhea. It can also temporarily or permanently affect fertility, depending on the patient's age, treatment type, dose, and duration; therefore, fertility preservation may need to be considered before starting treatment. A medical oncologist is the specialist who treats cancer with chemotherapy. If chemotherapy is planned as the first treatment, a biopsy is typically performed before therapy begins. Common chemotherapy drugs include platinum-based agents such as cisplatin and carboplatin, as well as 5-FU, mitomycin, capecitabine, oxaliplatin, irinotecan, and methotrexate.
Relationships:
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: NAUSEA
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens containing irinotecan tend to cause nausea
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: DIARRHEA
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens containing irinotecan tend to cause diarrhea</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
