    <node id="GENETIC TESTING">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Genetic testing is a medical procedure that analyzes genes or DNA to identify mutations, particularly those associated with cancer risk. It is closely related to biomarker testing and can be performed on blood, urine, saliva, or tumor samples to look for both inherited (germline) and tumor-related (somatic) mutations. When testing for inherited risk, it examines normal cells throughout the body to detect germline or inherited cancer-risk mutations, including in genes such as BRCA and other hereditary cancer genes. This type of testing is often recommended for individuals with a family history of cancer, and it is universally recommended for everyone diagnosed with ovarian cancer.

In clinical practice, genetic testing for inherited mutations is an important part of evaluating suspected ovarian cancer, often listed alongside family history. Patients are advised to know whether family members have undergone genetic testing, as well as the exact types and locations of cancers in their family, since this information can guide testing decisions. The care team typically asks whether family members have had genetic testing to better interpret results and assess hereditary risk. Testing for inherited genetic variants can be relevant when family members have had cancer, helping to identify individuals at increased risk.

In addition to inherited risk assessment, genetic testing may also be performed on a biopsy sample to help diagnose certain types of cancer, such as follicular or oncocytic thyroid cancer, when pathologic examination of removed tissue is not definitive. It is also used to test tumor tissue directly to identify mutations that may guide treatment decisions. The scope of genetic testing therefore encompasses both hereditary gene testing and tumor testing, covering germline and somatic alterations. By analyzing DNA from various sample types, genetic testing provides a comprehensive picture of cancer risk and tumor biology. It is a key tool for identifying inherited mutations that increase cancer risk and for informing personalized medical management. Overall, genetic testing serves multiple purposes: confirming hereditary cancer syndromes, guiding cancer diagnosis when standard pathology is inconclusive, and supporting treatment planning through tumor profiling. Because family history plays a crucial role, genetic testing is most effective when patients have accurate knowledge of their relatives' cancer diagnoses and prior genetic testing results.

Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: CARE TEAM
    Description: Genetic testing may be done through the gynecology or oncology care team
  - Source: CARE TEAM
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: The care team focuses on assessing cancer risk by inquiring about family history and the use of genetic testing. Specifically, they ask whether family members have undergone genetic testing, as this information helps clarify hereditary risk and guide patient care.
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: DOCTOR
    Description: Doctors use health history, family history, and genetic testing to assess hereditary ovarian cancer risk
  - Source: DOCTOR
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: The doctor asks whether family members had genetic testing
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: MUTATION
    Description: Genetic testing is done to look for inherited and tumor-related mutations
  - Source: MUTATION
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Genetic testing identifies mutations in genes
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: GERMLINE TESTING
    Description: Genetic testing is a broad term that encompasses various methods used to analyze an individual's DNA. Germline testing is a specific type of genetic testing that focuses on identifying inherited mutations. These mutations are present from birth and can be passed down from parents to their children. By looking for these inherited changes, germline testing helps assess an individual's risk for certain genetic conditions and can inform medical decisions.
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: GENETIC COUNSELOR
    Description: Genetic testing may be done through a genetic counselor, who helps patients understand the results
  - Source: GENETIC COUNSELOR
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: A genetic counselor helps interpret the results of genetic tests
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: BIOPSY
    Description: In tumor testing, a type of genetic testing, a biopsy sample is used for analysis
  - Source: BIOPSY
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Genetic testing can be performed on tumor tissue, and biopsy provides tissue samples for such testing.
  - Source: CANCER
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Genetic testing is suggested to find out if a person has inherited cancer risk
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: OVARIAN CANCER
    Description: Genetic testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with ovarian cancer
  - Source: OVARIAN CANCER
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Genetic testing is listed with family history as part of the evaluation for suspected ovarian cancer.
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Genetic testing results may guide cancer treatment
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: FAMILY HISTORY
    Description: Family history of cancer may make genetic testing important
  - Source: FAMILY HISTORY
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Family history of cancer may lead to genetic testing
  - Source: FINE-NEEDLE ASPIRATION
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Genetic testing can be performed on the biopsy sample obtained by FNA
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: MAINTENANCE THERAPY
    Description: BRCA status determined by genetic testing guides decisions about maintenance therapy.
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Description: Genetic testing can identify Lynch syndrome and other hereditary syndromes
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: TUMOR TESTING
    Description: Tumor testing is one of the two basic types of genetic testing for prostate cancer care
  - Source: FAMILY MEMBERS
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Family members may have undergone genetic testing, and this information is an important part of their health history. The patient should be aware of whether any relatives have had genetic testing, as it can provide valuable context for understanding inherited risks and guiding medical decisions.
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: BRCA MUTATION
    Description: Genetic testing can detect BRCA mutations in blood, saliva, cheek swab, or tumor tissue
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: HEREDITARY OVARIAN CANCER
    Description: Genetic testing can identify inherited mutations that cause hereditary ovarian cancer
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: HEREDITARY GENE TESTING
    Description: Hereditary gene testing is one of the two basic types of genetic testing for prostate cancer care
  - Source: BIOMARKER TESTING
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Biomarker testing may include genetic testing of tumor genes

