    <node id="PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The primary care physician (PCP) is the patient’s main and regular doctor, playing a central role in ongoing care. The PCP is considered a partner in the patient’s care and should be kept informed about any changes to the care team contact list, as well as any updates regarding the patient’s health, treatment, and important dates. In addition to providing general medical care, the PCP is often the first health care professional to evaluate people who have symptoms of anal cancer. Overall, the PCP serves as a key point of contact and coordinator within the patient’s broader health care team.
Relationships:
  - Source: HEALTH CARE PROVIDER
    Target: PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN
    Description: A primary care physician is a type of health care provider
  - Source: PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN
    Target: CARE TEAM
    Description: The primary care physician is part of the care team and should be kept informed about changes to the contact list
  - Source: CARE TEAM
    Target: PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN
    Description: The primary care physician is an integral member of the patient's care team and serves as an active partner in the patient's overall healthcare. In this role, the primary care physician collaborates with other care team members to ensure coordinated and comprehensive care. It is essential that the primary care physician remains informed of any updates or changes within the care team, including modifications to the team's contact list. This ensures effective communication and continuity of care, allowing the primary care physician to stay aligned with the team's evolving structure and patient needs. Thus, the relationship between the primary care physician and the care team is collaborative, with the physician both contributing to and being kept up to date on all relevant changes.
  - Source: PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN
    Target: PATIENT
    Description: The patient should keep their primary care physician informed of care team changes and regard the PCP as a partner in their care.
  - Source: PATIENT
    Target: PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN
    Description: The patient updates the primary care physician about any changes in health, treatment, and dates
  - Source: PRIMARY CARE PHYSICIAN
    Target: ANAL CANCER
    Description: Primary care physicians are often the first health care professionals patients with anal symptoms consult</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">6</data>
