    <node id="EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">No description provided in the Entities table. Relationship data describe it as a stage of prostate cancer defined by the cancer not having spread beyond the prostate gland (also called localized prostate cancer) .
Relationships:
  - Source: NCCN
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: NCCN produces a specific patient guideline for early-stage prostate cancer. 
  - Source: PROSTATE CANCER
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: Prostate cancer is a disease that originates in the prostate gland. It can be categorized into different stages based on how far it has progressed. Early-stage prostate cancer is one such category or stage of prostate cancer. This stage is defined by the fact that the cancer has not spread beyond the prostate gland. In other words, early-stage prostate cancer remains localized within the prostate, with no evidence of invasion into surrounding tissues or distant metastasis. As a subset of prostate cancer, early-stage prostate cancer represents a less advanced form of the disease, often associated with a better prognosis and more treatment options than later stages. Thus, while prostate cancer encompasses all stages of the disease, early-stage prostate cancer specifically refers to the confined, localized phase. 
  - Source: ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: Many patients with early-stage prostate cancer can be managed with active surveillance, which includes regular tests and no immediate treatment 
  - Source: ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: Early-stage and advanced-stage prostate cancer are the two main groupings of prostate cancer, distinguished by whether the cancer has spread beyond the prostate 
  - Source: LOCALIZED PROSTATE CANCER
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: Early-stage prostate cancer is also called localized prostate cancer; the two terms describe the same condition</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">5</data>
