Description: No description provided in the Entities table. Relationship data describe it as a stage of prostate cancer defined by the cancer not having spread beyond the prostate gland (also called localized prostate cancer) .
Relationships:
  - Source: NCCN
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: NCCN produces a specific patient guideline for early-stage prostate cancer. 
  - Source: PROSTATE CANCER
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: Prostate cancer is a disease that originates in the prostate gland. It can be categorized into different stages based on how far it has progressed. Early-stage prostate cancer is one such category or stage of prostate cancer. This stage is defined by the fact that the cancer has not spread beyond the prostate gland. In other words, early-stage prostate cancer remains localized within the prostate, with no evidence of invasion into surrounding tissues or distant metastasis. As a subset of prostate cancer, early-stage prostate cancer represents a less advanced form of the disease, often associated with a better prognosis and more treatment options than later stages. Thus, while prostate cancer encompasses all stages of the disease, early-stage prostate cancer specifically refers to the confined, localized phase. 
  - Source: ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: Many patients with early-stage prostate cancer can be managed with active surveillance, which includes regular tests and no immediate treatment 
  - Source: ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: Early-stage and advanced-stage prostate cancer are the two main groupings of prostate cancer, distinguished by whether the cancer has spread beyond the prostate 
  - Source: LOCALIZED PROSTATE CANCER
    Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: Early-stage prostate cancer is also called localized prostate cancer; the two terms describe the same condition</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOMARKER TESTING">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Biomarker testing is a laboratory diagnostic approach used to analyze tumor samples—typically from a biopsy, but sometimes from blood—for DNA mutations, protein levels, and other molecular features that may influence cancer treatment. Also known as molecular testing, tumor profiling, tumor sequencing, gene expression profiling, or genomic testing, it is designed to identify specific biomarkers associated with cancer. These biomarkers can include mutations, gene alterations, protein expression levels, and other molecular characteristics that help determine whether a patient is eligible for targeted therapies, immunotherapies, or clinical trials.

The test plays a key role in the evaluation and treatment planning of multiple cancer types, including nasopharyngeal carcinoma, ovarian cancer, recurrent cervical cancer, and other malignancies. In ovarian cancer, for example, biomarker testing is listed as part of the initial evaluation for suspected disease, looking for markers such as BRCA mutations, HRD status, HER2, MSI, MMR, TMB, BRAF, FRα, RET, and NTRK. For recurrent cervical cancer, molecular profiling of tumor tissue or blood is used to detect mutations such as PD-L1, MMR, MSI, TMB, NTRK, HER2, and RET, guiding targeted therapy or clinical trial eligibility.

Overall, biomarker testing provides essential information about the genetic and molecular makeup of a tumor, helping oncologists select the most effective treatment strategy. By analyzing genes, proteins, and other substances associated with cancer, it enables personalized medicine, ensuring that patients receive therapies most likely to benefit their specific tumor profile. The results can indicate whether a cancer has particular mutations or gene expression patterns that suggest responsiveness to certain drugs, thereby improving treatment decisions and potentially leading to better outcomes. In summary, biomarker testing is a foundational tool in modern cancer care, bridging laboratory analysis and clinical action to tailor treatment to the individual patient's disease.

Relationships:
  - Source: BIOMARKER TESTING
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: Biomarker testing may include genetic testing of tumor genes

---</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
