Description: A radiologist is a physician (doctor) who specializes in interpreting medical imaging tests. They are experts in reading and analyzing images from a wide range of modalities, including MRI, CT, PET, ultrasound, and bone scans, as well as diagnostic mammograms. In some settings, a radiologist evaluates diagnostic mammograms while the patient waits. They study the images carefully, write a detailed report of their findings, and send the results to the patient’s doctor, health care provider, or care team—such as an oncologist. Some radiologists have particular expertise in imaging the nervous system, reviewing MRI or CT images of the brain and spine and conveying results to the care team. Overall, the radiologist serves as a diagnostic expert who bridges imaging technology and clinical care, ensuring that referring physicians receive accurate interpretations to guide patient treatment.
**Relationships:** *(No relationships listed in the provided data.)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER (ID 35)">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER is a medical professional who delivers comprehensive patient care. This clinician, which includes doctors and other medical professionals, performs thorough physical examinations and checks vital signs, body systems, and lymph nodes. The health care provider also assesses height, weight, lungs, heart, eyes, ears, nose, throat, organs, and skin. In addition to conducting exams, the provider gives patients important information about next steps and treatment options. The health care provider (HCP) receives imaging reports from the radiologist and discusses the results with the patient, ensuring clear communication and follow-up care.
**Relationships:**
  - **Source:** HEALTH CARE PROVIDER
    **Target:** PHYSICIAN
    **Description:** A physician is a type of health care provider.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
