    <node id="IMMUNOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Immunotherapy is a type of systemic cancer treatment that uses the body’s immune system to find and destroy cancer cells by increasing immune system activity. As a systemic therapy, it treats cancer cells throughout the body and can be used in various clinical situations. Immunotherapy may be recommended for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) that has spread, particularly when surgery and radiation are not possible or have been unsuccessful. It is also the preferred treatment for dMMR/MSI-H cancers, often using checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab, nivolumab with or without ipilimumab. For metastatic anal cancer that does not respond to treatment or worsens, immunotherapy with an immune checkpoint inhibitor is recommended; in some anal cancer patients, it has worked well enough that surgery was no longer needed. Additionally, immunotherapy can be used before surgery to make the procedure less risky. It is also considered when cancer progresses during or after chemotherapy.

The main immunotherapy drug for primary CNS lymphoma is rituximab, a monoclonal antibody that attaches to CD20 proteins on cancer cells. Immunotherapy includes PD-1 inhibitors and other checkpoint inhibitors. Possible side effects of immunotherapy include autoimmune reactions, fatigue, fever, nausea, loss of appetite, infection, diarrhea, and constipation. Overall, immunotherapy is a versatile systemic cancer treatment that harnesses the immune system, with specific agents and indications varying by cancer type. 
Relationships:
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
    Target: Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) that has spread
    Description: Immunotherapy may be recommended for CSCC that has spread, particularly when surgery and radiation are not possible or have been unsuccessful. 
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
    Target: dMMR/MSI-H cancers
    Description: Immunotherapy is the preferred treatment for dMMR/MSI-H cancers, often using checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab, nivolumab with or without ipilimumab. 
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
    Target: Metastatic anal cancer
    Description: For metastatic anal cancer that does not respond to treatment or worsens, immunotherapy with an immune checkpoint inhibitor is recommended. 
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
    Target: Primary CNS lymphoma
    Description: The main immunotherapy drug for primary CNS lymphoma is rituximab, a monoclonal antibody that attaches to CD20 proteins on cancer cells. 

---</data>
