    <node id="DIARRHEA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">DIARRHEA is a common side effect associated with several cancer treatment modalities. Specifically, it frequently occurs as a result of external beam radiation therapy, particularly when radiation is directed at the abdomen or pelvis. Additionally, diarrhea is a well-documented adverse effect of targeted therapy, a class of drugs designed to interfere with specific molecules involved in tumor growth. Furthermore, the chemotherapy agent irinotecan is known to commonly cause diarrhea as a side effect. In summary, diarrhea is a shared and clinically significant side effect across external beam radiation therapy, targeted therapy, and treatment with irinotecan.
Relationships:
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: DIARRHEA
  - Description: Diarrhea is a common side effect of EBRT.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: DIARRHEA
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens containing irinotecan tend to cause diarrhea
  - Source: TARGETED THERAPY
  - Target: DIARRHEA
  - Description: Diarrhea is a common side effect of targeted therapy</data>
