    <node id="SURGERY (ID: 46)">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">SURGERY is a cancer treatment modality involving the operative removal or repair of cells, tissues, organs, or tumors, performed by surgical specialists such as surgical oncologists, gynecologic oncologists, neurosurgeons, and plastic surgeons. It is used to remove cancer, evaluate lymph nodes, provide tissue for pathologic staging, and sometimes reconstruct affected areas. Tissue removed during surgery is examined by a pathologist; staging may be performed before and after surgery.

As a main treatment for early-stage cervical cancer, surgery includes laparotomy, minimally invasive surgery, cone biopsy, trachelectomy, hysterectomy, and lymph node dissection, typically performed by a gynecologic oncologist. For ovarian cancer, testing helps determine whether surgery first is possible based on tumor size, location, and health; surgery removes tumors, provides tissue for testing, and may be the first treatment, with biopsy often performed during initial surgery. Chemotherapy may be given before or after, and the ovarian capsule may break, causing a surgical spill. For breast cancer, surgery may be the initial treatment and provides tissue for pathologic staging; procedures include mastectomy, lumpectomy, open biopsy, lymph node removal, and reconstruction, though surgery may not always be possible. For prostate cancer, surgery may be avoided or delayed if molecular biomarker testing indicates lower-risk cancer. For rectal cancer, MRI is ordered first if surgery is being considered to determine how deep the cancer has grown and whether it has spread. For colon cancer, surgery removes the cancer and may be performed first or after colostomy/stent; if it cannot be done first, systemic therapy is given first, and follow-up colonoscopy is recommended after surgery for stage 1 disease.

For squamous cell skin cancer (including CSCC and SISCCA), surgery is the main treatment for local and regional disease; it aims to remove all cancer while sparing surrounding skin, using PDEMA/Mohs procedure, standard surgical excision, skin grafts, or flaps, and may include removal of nearby lymph nodes. If a SISCCA lesion is completely removed, further treatment may not be needed. For brain tumors, a neurosurgeon performs surgery in an operating room, including biopsy, open biopsy, and resection. Head and neck cancer surgery is complex and requires preparation for complications, especially in patients with other health conditions or airway/swallowing issues; whether a tumor is resectable can be determined by direct laryngoscopy, and surgical samples are sent for cytology/pathology. For thyroid cancer, surgery is the most common and most effective treatment, involving removal of part or all of the thyroid gland and/or lymph nodes. For bladder cancer, surgery ranges from removing a single tumor from the bladder lining to removing the entire bladder and other organs. For perianal cancer, local excision is the specific surgical option for early disease. Surgery is also a past medical treatment asked about when planning CLL treatment.

Smoking, vaping, or cannabis use during and after surgery can increase side effects or affect anesthesia.

Relationships:
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: NEUROSURGEON
  - Description: The neurosurgeon performs surgery in the operating room to remove tumor tissue. (Relationship id: 1587)

  - Source: PLASTIC SURGEON
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Plastic surgeons perform surgery to remove cancer and to reconstruct, restore, and repair body parts (Relationship id: 2020)

  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: SURGEON
  - Description: A surgeon performs surgery. (Relationship id: 1928)

  - Source: SURGICAL ONCOLOGIST
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Surgical oncologists perform surgery to diagnose and remove cancerous tumors (Relationship id: 2015)

  - Source: HEAD AND NECK SURGEON
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Head and neck surgeons are surgical specialists who treat conditions of the deeper head and neck, including the ears, nose, and throat (Relationship id: 2023)

---</data>
