    <node id="OPEN BIOPSY">
      <data key="d3">9</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Open biopsy is a surgical biopsy procedure in which a neurosurgeon performs a craniotomy, opening the scalp and skull to remove pieces of tumor tissue. It is one of the two standard types of biopsies used for gliomas, and it serves as a surgical procedure to excise a tumor in order to confirm its histology.
Relationships:
  - Source: BIOPSY
    Target: OPEN BIOPSY
    Description: Open biopsy is a type of biopsy, which is a medical procedure used to obtain tissue samples for diagnosis. Specifically, open biopsy is recognized as one of the standard types of biopsy for gliomas, a form of brain tumor. Thus, within the broader category of biopsy, open biopsy holds particular importance in the diagnostic evaluation of gliomas.
  - Source: OPEN BIOPSY
    Target: BIOPSY
    Description: Open biopsy is one type of biopsy procedure.
  - Source: OPEN BIOPSY
    Target: SURGERY
    Description: Open biopsy is a surgical procedure. It is specifically performed to remove a tumor for diagnostic evaluation. As a form of surgery, an open biopsy involves the direct excision of tissue, allowing for pathological examination.
  - Source: OPEN BIOPSY
    Target: NEUROSURGEON
    Description: The neurosurgeon performs an open biopsy by opening the scalp and skull to remove tumor pieces.
  - Source: OPEN BIOPSY
    Target: NEUROPATHOLOGIST
    Description: Tissue from an open biopsy is examined by the neuropathologist.
  - Source: OPEN BIOPSY
    Target: RESECTION
    Description: An open biopsy is described as being like a resection, both being surgical procedures involving tumor removal.
  - Source: OPEN BIOPSY
    Target: LABORATORY
    Description: After an open biopsy, the tissue sample is sent to a laboratory for analysis.
  - Source: OPEN BIOPSY
    Target: HISTOLOGY
    Description: Open biopsy may be recommended to confirm histology.
  - Source: STEREOTACTIC BIOPSY
    Target: OPEN BIOPSY
    Description: They are the two types of standard biopsies for gliomas.

---</data>
