**Summary of key findings:** The data identify stereotactic biopsy as a minimally invasive, image-guided (MRI or CT) surgical procedure performed by a neurosurgeon in an operating room, using a frame or skin markers, a navigation system, and a thin hollow needle to sample brain lesions — particularly gliomas in hard-to-reach or vital areas. The sample is sent to a laboratory, where a neuropathologist determines whether the tumor is benign or malignant, its type and grade, and molecular features. The provided data list infection, rectal bleeding, and blood in urine, stool, or semen as general biopsy complications; hemorrhage is not explicitly named in the data .</data>
