    <node id="NAUSEA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Nausea is a common side effect associated with several cancer treatments. It frequently occurs as a result of external beam radiation therapy, a standard form of radiotherapy. Beyond this specific technique, nausea is also a common side effect of radiation therapy in general, as well as targeted therapy. Additionally, nausea is a side effect that the chemotherapy drug irinotecan tends to cause. Thus, patients undergoing external beam radiation, broader radiation therapy, targeted therapy, or treatment with irinotecan may experience nausea as an adverse effect.
Relationships:
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: NAUSEA
  - Description: Nausea is a common side effect of EBRT.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: NAUSEA
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens containing irinotecan tend to cause nausea
  - Source: TARGETED THERAPY
  - Target: NAUSEA
  - Description: Nausea is a common side effect of targeted therapy
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: NAUSEA
  - Description: Nausea is a common side effect of radiation therapy</data>
