For bladder cancer, EBRT is the type of radiation therapy usually used; a large machine aims radiation at the tumor area.

For breast cancer, EBRT uses a machine called a linear accelerator to aim radiation at the whole breast and lymph nodes.

Overall, external beam radiation therapy is a versatile, non-invasive cancer treatment that delivers precise radiation from outside the body to destroy or shrink tumors. It is used for a variety of cancers, with treatment schedules and side effects depending on the specific cancer type and location. 
Relationships:
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
    Target: Cervical cancer
    Description: EBRT is a standard treatment option for cervical cancer that aims radiation at the cancer site and nearby lymph nodes, often given in fractions over 5 to 6 weeks. 
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
    Target: Rectal cancer
    Description: For rectal cancer, external beam radiation therapy uses radiation to treat the tumor, but it can also damage the ovaries and reduce hormones needed for natural pregnancy. 
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
    Target: Bladder cancer
    Description: For bladder cancer, EBRT is the type of radiation therapy usually used; a large machine aims radiation at the tumor area. 
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
    Target: Breast cancer
    Description: For breast cancer, EBRT uses a machine called a linear accelerator to aim radiation at the whole breast and lymph nodes. 

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GERMLINE MUTATION">
