    <node id="LYNCH SYNDROME">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition caused by mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes. It is a hereditary disorder that significantly increases the risk of developing several types of cancer, most notably colon cancer, as well as uterine (endometrial), ovarian, and other cancers. Lynch syndrome can also cause hereditary ovarian cancer and may increase the risk of developing an adrenal tumor. Because of its strong hereditary nature and elevated cancer risks, Lynch syndrome is recognized as a factor that may make germline genetic testing appropriate for affected individuals and their families. 
Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Description: Relation type: identifies — Genetic testing can identify Lynch syndrome and other hereditary syndromes. 
  - Source: HEREDITARY OVARIAN CANCER
    Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Description: Relation type: is caused by — Lynch syndrome can cause hereditary ovarian cancer. 
  - Source: OVARIAN CANCER
    Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Description: Relation type: is caused by — Lynch syndrome is a cause of ovarian cancer in addition to colon and uterine cancers. 
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Target: GERMLINE TESTING
    Description: Relation type: indicates/justifies — Lynch syndrome is listed as a factor making germline testing more appropriate. 
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Target: ADRENAL TUMORS
    Description: Relation type: predisposes to — Lynch syndrome is a hereditary risk factor for developing an adrenal tumor. 
  - Source: COLON CANCER
    Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Description: Relation type: is associated with — Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the disease’s onset. 
  - Source: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
    Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Description: Relation type: leads to testing for — If colon cancer is dMMR/MSI-H, mismatch repair testing leads to testing for Lynch syndrome. 
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Target: GENETIC COUNSELING
    Description: Relation type: indicates/leads to — Lynch syndrome is a factor that should lead a person to ask about genetic counseling.</data>
