    <node id="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is a hereditary condition and an inherited syndrome that significantly increases the risk of developing an adrenal tumor. It is also closely related to colon cancer, as it is an inherited syndrome that causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum. These polyps can eventually lead to invasive colon cancer if left untreated. Thus, FAP is a genetic disorder characterized by the development of numerous colorectal polyps and an elevated risk for both adrenal tumors and invasive colorectal cancer. 
Relationships:
  - Source: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS
    Target: ADRENAL TUMORS
    Description: Relation type: predisposes to — Familial adenomatous polyposis is a hereditary risk factor for developing an adrenal tumor. 
  - Source: COLON CANCER
    Target: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS
    Description: Relation type: is associated with — Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited syndrome closely related to colon cancer. In classic FAP, the polyps characteristic of the condition can become invasive colon cancer, often by age 50. Thus, colon cancer is a major complication of this inherited syndrome, with FAP serving as a direct precursor condition. 
  - Source: POLYPS
    Target: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS
    Description: Relation type: is caused by — FAP causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum.</data>
