    <node id="MAINTENANCE THERAPY">
      <data key="d3">8</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">MAINTENANCE THERAPY is a treatment approach that involves continued or follow-up care administered after the completion of initial therapy, with the primary goal of preventing or reducing the risk of disease recurrence. It is not a single fixed regimen but rather a concept applied across different medical contexts, particularly in oncology.

In the context of bladder cancer, maintenance therapy is a follow-up regimen of intravesical BCG (bacillus Calmette-Guérin) treatments given at intervals after a transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT). This approach is designed to prevent bladder cancer recurrence by delivering immunotherapy directly into the bladder on a scheduled basis.

More generally, maintenance therapy is defined as continued treatment given after initial therapy to reduce the likelihood of recurrence. For example, it may be administered every six months for up to three years, depending on the condition and the treatment plan.

In certain cancers, such as ovarian cancer, maintenance therapy may be offered once the disease is in remission, as a way to extend the period of remission and delay potential relapse. Decisions about whether to pursue maintenance therapy in these cases are often guided by specific biomarkers; for instance, BRCA status may influence the choice of maintenance treatment, as certain therapies are more effective in patients with BRCA mutations.

Thus, while the specific drugs, schedules, and indications vary, the unifying principle of maintenance therapy is its role as a proactive, long-term follow-up strategy after initial successful treatment, aimed at sustaining remission and minimizing the chance of recurrence across different cancer types.

