    <node id="SMOKING">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Smoking is a behavior involving the use of tobacco, and it is recognized as a significant lifestyle-related factor that increases the risk of several types of cancer, including anal cancer and bladder cancer. Beyond its role in cancer development, smoking—or having a history of smoking—also negatively affects cancer treatment outcomes. It can make cancer treatment less effective and increases the risk of surgical side effects. However, quitting smoking can lead to better cancer treatment outcomes, highlighting the importance of smoking cessation for improved prognosis and overall health.
Relationships:
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Smoking can limit how well cancer treatment works</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">14</data>
