Recurrence is described specifically for several cancers: it is the return of bladder cancer after treatment; the return of cervical cancer after treatment, which may be persistent, local/regional, or distant and is investigated with imaging, blood tests, and biomarker testing; the return of basal cell skin cancer to the original area or its spread; and the possible return of prostate cancer after treatment. For cancers such as anal cancer, treatment depends on whether the recurrence appears in the anus, nearby lymph nodes, or distant sites. In some cancers, recurrence status determines whether the disease is considered platinum-resistant or platinum-sensitive, which directly guides subsequent treatment. Overall, recurrence represents the reappearance of cancer after initial treatment, and its management is tailored to the specific cancer type, location, extent, and biological behavior.

