    <node id="TREATMENT (ENTITY 36)">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">TREATMENT refers to the broad range of medical care and interventions provided to cancer patients after diagnosis. It encompasses standard-of-care therapies such as surgery, radiation therapy, chemotherapy, targeted therapy, systemic therapy, endocrine therapy, stem cell or hematopoietic cell transplant, and participation in clinical trials, as well as supportive care. The primary goals of treatment are to cure cancer, put it into remission, slow disease progression, reduce symptoms, and help people live longer.

Treatment is planned and delivered by the care team based on a thorough risk assessment, the extent of cancer, and diagnostic testing. Tests such as blood tests, bone marrow aspirate and biopsy, and imaging studies are used to plan treatment, evaluate how well treatment is working, and monitor a patient’s health after treatment ends. Imaging may also be used before, during, or after treatment to help plan care, assess response, and check for recurrence. For lower-risk cases—particularly in prostate cancer and basal cell skin cancer—treatment may be avoided or delayed, with some patients requiring minimal or no intervention. Conversely, higher-risk or more aggressive disease usually requires more intensive treatment.

Treatment plans are often delivered in phases, as seen in acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where systemic therapy, chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and hematopoietic cell transplant are administered in stages with the goal of achieving remission. The approach for cancers such as cervical cancer may include surgery, radiation therapy, chemoradiation, systemic therapy, and clinical trials, depending on how far the cancer has spread. Bladder cancer treatment may be stronger or more intense than other options, while prostate cancer management focuses on slowing the disease, reducing symptoms, and prolonging survival.

Regardless of cancer type, treatment can have significant side effects, including effects on fertility, hearing, endocrine glands, and a developing baby. The potential for toxicity and long-term outcomes are important considerations when selecting a treatment plan. Overall, treatment is the comprehensive management of cancer, integrating therapeutic procedures, supportive care, and ongoing monitoring to optimize patient outcomes throughout the cancer journey.
Relationships:
  - Source: TREATMENT
    Target: CARE TEAM
    Description: The care team is central to the entire cancer treatment process, particularly for patients with acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Their responsibilities begin with planning the next steps of care, which are determined based on imaging and test results. Using these findings, the care team identifies the appropriate course of action and outlines the treatment pathway.

The care team provides treatment options and comprehensive care to the patient. Treatment planning is a collaborative process: the patient and care team together choose a treatment plan that aligns with the patient’s needs and preferences. Once a plan is agreed upon, the care team coordinates care throughout the entire treatment journey, ensuring that all aspects of the plan are implemented smoothly.

In addition to planning, the care team actively prescribes and monitors treatments. They ensure that each treatment is properly initiated, and they list the start date of each treatment. This monitoring is essential for tracking the patient’s response and adjusting the plan as needed. Ultimately, the care team provides the planned cancer treatment itself, delivering the therapies that have been selected for the patient. Through this combination of planning, prescribing, monitoring, coordinating, and providing care, the care team manages the full continuum of AML treatment, supporting the patient from initial decision-making through ongoing therapy. (Relationship 174)
  - Source: TREATMENT
    Target: PATIENT
    Description: The patient may avoid or delay treatment if biomarker testing shows lower-risk cancer. (Relationship 1801)
  - Source: PATIENT
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: The patient undergoes cancer treatment, which may affect fertility, hearing, and other functions. (Relationship 82)
  - Source: TREATMENT
    Target: TREATMENT PLANNING
    Description: Treatment planning decides the treatment options for the patient. (Relationship 2667)
  - Source: TREATMENT PLANNING
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Treatment planning determines the treatment course. (Relationship 172)
  - Source: BIOPSY
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Biopsy results and biopsy findings play a central role in guiding cancer treatment decisions. After a cancer diagnosis, these results are used to help plan the appropriate treatment approach. Additionally, biopsy findings provide specific information about which treatments the cancer may respond to, enabling more targeted and effective therapy. Together, biopsy and treatment are directly connected: the biopsy supplies essential diagnostic and predictive data, while treatment planning relies on that data to determine the best course of action for each patient. (Relationship 1910)
  - Source: TREATMENT
    Target: SURGERY
    Description: Surgery is a type of cancer treatment. (Relationship 88)
  - Source: SURGERY
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Surgery is a type of treatment, specifically a form of cancer treatment. It is one of the main treatment options available for cancer, including cervical cancer, where it is recognized as a primary treatment approach. Surgery can also be combined with other therapies; when drug therapy is administered before surgery, the pathologic stage of the disease may include a "y" prefix to indicate this sequence. As a treatment modality, surgery plays a key role in the management of cancer and is included among the main types of treatment for cervical cancer. (Relationship 1142)
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Treatment is given for cervical cancer, and options depend on the extent of the cancer and other factors. (Relationship 2351)
  - Source: TREATMENT
    Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
    Description: Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiation—a combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens. (Relationship 86)
  - Source: TREATMENT
    Target: RADIATION THERAPY
    Description: Radiation therapy is a type of treatment that falls under the broader category of medical treatments. Specifically, it is a type of cancer treatment, used to address malignant diseases. Additionally, radiation therapy is a type of treatment for cervical cancer, indicating its application in managing that particular form of cancer. (Relationship 87)
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Radiation therapy is a type of treatment. (Relationship 1792)
  - Source: IMAGING TESTS
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Imaging helps plan treatment and is used after treatment to evaluate results and check for recurrence. (Relationship 3010)
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: A clinical trial is a treatment option that may be considered for a patient. Clinical trials offer potential new therapies and serve as another treatment option. (Relationship 1929)
  - Source: TREATMENT
    Target: METASTASIS
    Description: Treatment is evaluated in metastatic cancer, and targeted therapy may be used for certain metastatic cases. (Relationship 3028)
  - Source: METASTASIS
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Metastatic disease affects treatment planning and may be classified as very advanced disease. (Relationship 2213)
  - Source: BLOOD TESTS
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Blood tests are used to check for signs of disease and how well organs are working during treatment planning and monitoring. (Relationship 1963)
  - Source: CHEMORADIATION
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Chemoradiation is a type of treatment for cervical cancer. (Relationship 2377)
  - Source: TREATMENT
    Target: PROSTATE CANCER
