    <node id="STAGE 2 OR 3 RECTAL CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d4"> — Chemoradiation is a possible next (adjunct) treatment for stage 2 or 3 rectal cancer. After transabdominal surgery, the stage is used to determine whether more treatment is needed; for stage 2 and 3 rectal cancers, next treatments may include chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both. Therapeutic relation: **treats/manages** (adjunct) [Data: Entities (146); Sources (206, 207)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RECTAL CANCER AFTER LOCAL EXCISION WITH HIGH-RISK FEATURES OR T2 TUMORS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4"> — When local excision results show high-risk features or a larger tumor invading the muscle layer, the preferred next treatment is transabdominal surgery, but a second option is chemotherapy combined with radiation (chemoradiation). This approach is normally used for locally advanced cancers. If chemoradiation works well, subsequent management may include further chemotherapy or a watch-and-wait approach; if signs of cancer remain, transabdominal resection is recommended. Therapeutic relation: **treats/manages** (adjunct/second option) [Data: Entities (146); Sources (206)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER FOUND IN LYMPH NODES OR TISSUES OUTSIDE THE CERVIX">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d4"> — The chemoradiation approach recommended for locally advanced cervical cancer is also recommended for cancer found in lymph nodes or tissues outside the cervix. Therapeutic relation: **treats** [Data: Entities (146)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOCALLY ADVANCED CANCERS (GENERAL)">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4"> — More broadly, chemoradiation is used for locally advanced cancers and may be offered as a second option after local excision when additional treatment is needed. It may also be given after certain chemotherapy regimens. Therapeutic relation: **treats/manages** [Data: Entities (146); Sources (206)].

After completing chemoradiation, initial follow-up evaluation and surveillance are recommended for these cancers [Data: Entities (146)].

---
</data>
    </node>
