    <node id="EXCISIONAL BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Excisional biopsy is a surgical procedure in which an entire abnormal area, lesion, or suspicious tissue is removed for diagnostic testing. It is a specific type of biopsy that may be necessary when other, less invasive biopsy methods cannot be used or are insufficient to provide a definitive diagnosis. In the context of lymph node evaluation, excisional biopsy involves the surgical removal of all or part of a lymph node for pathological examination. This approach allows pathologists to examine the complete structure and margins of the removed tissue, providing comprehensive information about the nature of the abnormality, such as whether it is benign or malignant. Because the entire abnormal area is excised, this technique can sometimes serve both diagnostic and therapeutic purposes, particularly when the lesion is small and completely removed. However, it is generally reserved for cases where other sampling methods, such as fine-needle aspiration or core needle biopsy, are not suitable, inconclusive, or cannot safely reach the tissue in question.
Relationships: None recorded in the provided Relationships table.

---</data>
