    <node id="GENITAL WARTS — &quot;CAUSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
There is **no formal relationship record** in the Relationships table directly connecting HPV INFECTION to GENITAL WARTS. However, the causal link is supported by entity descriptions and source text. The HPV INFECTION entity states it "can sometimes cause abnormal tissue growth" [Data: Entities (66)], and the HPV entity states that persistent infection "can also cause other cancers and genital warts" [Data: Entities (918)]. The source text further says "HPV infections can cause abnormal tissue growth (warts) and sometimes cancer" and "Other HPV types can cause abnormal skin growths, called warts, to form on the anus, genitals, or other areas of the body" [Data: Sources (4, 168)].

**Relation type: causes (supported by entity/source text, but no formal Relationships-table record exists between these two entities).**
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV VACCINE — &quot;PREVENTED_BY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
This relationship **is formally supported**. The data records that "The HPV vaccine prevents new HPV infections" [Data: Relationships (2251, 197)]. Both the HPV VACCINATION and VACCINATION entities confirm the vaccine "can prevent new HPV infections but does not treat existing HPV infection or HPV-related cancer" [Data: Entities (926, 105)]. Note that the dataset contains the entities **HPV VACCINATION** and **VACCINATION**, not an entity literally named "HPV VACCINE."

**Relation type: prevented_by (formally supported).**
</data>
    </node>
