    <edge source="SURGERY" target="PDEMA/MOHS PROCEDURE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">PDEMA/Mohs procedure is a surgical procedure performed to remove skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery is a type of treatment, specifically a form of cancer treatment. It is one of the main treatment options available for cancer, including cervical cancer, where it is recognized as a primary treatment approach. Surgery can also be combined with other therapies; when drug therapy is administered before surgery, the pathologic stage of the disease may include a "y" prefix to indicate this sequence. As a treatment modality, surgery plays a key role in the management of cancer and is included among the main types of treatment for cervical cancer. (Relationship 1142)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery and cancer treatment are closely interconnected, with surgery serving as a major therapeutic approach in oncology. Surgery is a form of cancer treatment that is used to remove cancerous tissue from the body. It is also one component of a broader cancer treatment plan, which may include other modalities such as radiation therapy, chemotherapy, or immunotherapy. In the context of head and neck cancers specifically, surgery is considered a complex form of cancer treatment due to the intricate anatomy of the region and the need for precise removal of tumors while preserving critical functions like breathing, swallowing, and speech. Additionally, discussions of surgery as a cancer treatment include attention to surgery side effects in cancer care, acknowledging that the procedure can have physical and functional impacts that must be managed before, during, and after treatment. Thus, surgery is both a distinct, direct method for excising cancer and an integral part of multidisciplinary cancer care, with its own risks and benefits that are weighed carefully by the medical team.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
