    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The treatment team explains how often, how long, and how chemotherapy is given and its side effects</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy and immunotherapy are both systemic cancer treatments, meaning they travel through the bloodstream to affect cells throughout the body. They are commonly used together or as separate options depending on the cancer type and its molecular characteristics. For instance, in certain cancers, chemotherapy is recommended for tumors that are pMMR/MSS (mismatch repair-proficient/microsatellite stable), while immunotherapy is preferred for tumors that are dMMR/MSI-H (mismatch repair-deficient/microsatellite instability-high). Both treatments are also standard systemic therapy options for bladder cancer. In the context of metastatic anal cancer, immunotherapy is specifically recommended when the disease does not respond to, or worsens after, chemotherapy. Despite their different mechanismschemotherapy directly targets rapidly dividing cells, while immunotherapy harnesses the immune systemthe two approaches share several similar side effects, including nausea, fever, infection, diarrhea, and fatigue. Overall, chemotherapy and immunotherapy play distinct but complementary roles in cancer care, with the choice of treatment guided by tumor biology, cancer type, and response to prior therapies.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="DIARRHEA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy regimens containing irinotecan tend to cause diarrhea</data>
      <data key="d8">DIARRHEA</data>
    </edge>
