    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="RADIATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">When chemotherapy is combined with radiation, it is called chemoradiation; chemotherapy may also be given after chemoradiation to kill lingering cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="ACUTE MYELOID LEUKEMIA (AML)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is the standard-of-care systemic treatment for AML, where it kills fast-dividing cells and is often multi-agent and given in cycles.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a main treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, with methotrexate being the principal drug; it can be combined with targeted therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CUTANEOUS SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA (CSCC)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">For CSCC that has spread, chemotherapy may be used when surgery and radiation are not possible or unsuccessful.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="COLORECTAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is recommended after colectomy for all stage 3 and some stage 2 colon cancers; rectal cancer regimens may include oxaliplatin or irinotecan.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy may be given first when surgery is not initially possible (with preferred regimens including paclitaxel and carboplatin), or after surgery to address residual disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">For cervical cancer, chemotherapy is combined with radiation as chemoradiation, often given once weekly.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="BLADDER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is one of the main intravesical therapies for bladder cancer (delivered directly into the bladder), and may also be given systemically before or after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="NAUSEA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy regimens containing irinotecan tend to cause nausea</data>
      <data key="d8">NAUSEA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment and one component of the broader category of cancer treatment. It is used to combat cancer but can have side effects, notably affecting fertility. Thus, while chemotherapy is a key part of cancer treatment, it is not the entirety of it, and its impact on fertility is an important consideration for patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A biopsy is needed to confirm ovarian cancer before chemotherapy is started</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients should talk to their treatment team about the benefits of radiation therapy compared with the risk of nervous system damage</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is typically given Monday through Friday for 5 to 6 weeks as part of chemoradiation for anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="ANAL CANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a component of chemoradiation for non-metastatic anal canal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a type of treatment. (Relationship 1792)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy may be combined with chemotherapy (a combination called chemoradiation) to enhance its effect.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a treatment option for prostate cancer, and may be avoided or delayed if molecular biomarker testing indicates lower-risk disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="RECTAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is often used for rectal cancer and can damage the hormone-making function of the ovaries and testicles, potentially causing infertility.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">For cervical cancer, radiation therapy is a treatment category that includes external beam radiation therapy and brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="SQUAMOUS CELL SKIN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a treatment option for squamous cell skin cancer when surgery is not an option, after surgery, or with systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="THYROID CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is listed as a treatment for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a main treatment for primary CNS lymphoma that can slow tumor growth and reduce symptoms; whole-brain radiation therapy is the most commonly used technique.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="HEAD AND NECK CANCERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy can affect endocrine glands such as the thyroid and pituitary, as well as hearing, and may require related assessments before or after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="NAUSEA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Nausea is a common side effect of radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d8">NAUSEA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
