      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a type of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A health care provider gives information about next steps and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient may avoid or delay treatment if biomarker testing shows lower-risk cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="TARGETED THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Targeted therapy is a form of cancer treatment that specifically targets the molecular mechanisms driving cancer growth. It is also a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), commonly described as a type of AML treatment or a treatment option for AML. In the context of AML, targeted therapy works by interfering with specific genetic mutations, proteins, or pathways that contribute to the development and survival of leukemic cells, thereby distinguishing it from traditional chemotherapy. Thus, both as a general cancer treatment and as a specific AML treatment, targeted therapy plays a critical role in offering more precise, often less toxic, therapeutic choices for patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">TARGETED THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team is central to the entire cancer treatment process, particularly for patients with acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Their responsibilities begin with planning the next steps of care, which are determined based on imaging and test results. Using these findings, the care team identifies the appropriate course of action and outlines the treatment pathway.

The care team provides treatment options and comprehensive care to the patient. Treatment planning is a collaborative process: the patient and care team together choose a treatment plan that aligns with the patients needs and preferences. Once a plan is agreed upon, the care team coordinates care throughout the entire treatment journey, ensuring that all aspects of the plan are implemented smoothly.

In addition to planning, the care team actively prescribes and monitors treatments. They ensure that each treatment is properly initiated, and they list the start date of each treatment. This monitoring is essential for tracking the patients response and adjusting the plan as needed. Ultimately, the care team provides the planned cancer treatment itself, delivering the therapies that have been selected for the patient. Through this combination of planning, prescribing, monitoring, coordinating, and providing care, the care team manages the full continuum of AML treatment, supporting the patient from initial decision-making through ongoing therapy. (Relationship 174)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="TREATMENT PLANNING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment planning decides the treatment options for the patient. (Relationship 2667)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surgery is a type of cancer treatment. (Relationship 88)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiationa combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens. (Relationship 86)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a type of treatment that falls under the broader category of medical treatments. Specifically, it is a type of cancer treatment, used to address malignant diseases. Additionally, radiation therapy is a type of treatment for cervical cancer, indicating its application in managing that particular form of cancer. (Relationship 87)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment is evaluated in metastatic cancer, and targeted therapy may be used for certain metastatic cases. (Relationship 3028)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment is planned and given to manage prostate cancer. (Relationship 3000)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer treatment is a specific type of treatment. (Relationship 1868)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="DOCTOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The doctor explains medical information, including treatment plans, and should be asked for simpler terms when needed. (Relationship 955)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic therapy is a major type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML). As a treatment approach, systemic therapy works throughout the body, distinguishing it from localized treatments. It is specifically recognized as a type of AML treatment, meaning it is used to address the disease on a systemic level. Systemic therapy is therefore an important component of the broader treatment landscape for AML, offering a method that reaches cancer cells regardless of their location in the body. (Relationship 949)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Primary CNS lymphoma can be treated to slow growth, reduce symptoms, and prolong life. (Relationship 2716)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="BLADDER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment is provided for bladder cancer. (Relationship 1342)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="RECURRENCE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment and recurrence are closely linked concepts in cancer care. After a patient completes treatment, follow-up imaging is commonly used to monitor for any signs that the cancer has returned, a process known as recurrence. This surveillance helps detect whether the cancer has come back after initial therapy. Additionally, risk assessment plays a key role in evaluating the likelihood of recurrence following treatment, as well as determining whether a recurrence can be effectively managed with an alternative treatment approach. Together, these descriptions highlight that treatment is not only aimed at eliminating cancer but also involves ongoing monitoring and risk evaluation to address the possibility of recurrence. (Relationship 1824)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials are a type of treatment option, specifically one that may be recommended for patients with acute myeloid leukemia (AML). As a treatment approach, clinical trials offer eligible individuals access to investigational therapies and contribute to the advancement of medical knowledge. (Relationship 951)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CANCER STAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment decisions are guided by cancer stage, and treatment may begin after staging is initially determined. (Relationship 2214)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="PREGNANCY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer treatment can hurt the developing baby if the patient is or becomes pregnant during treatment. (Relationship 89)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supportive care is a type of treatment and care option, particularly used for acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Its primary purpose is to help manage the side effects of the disease and its treatment, aiming to improve the patient's quality of life and overall well-being. As a form of treatment, supportive care works alongside other medical interventions to address complications, reduce symptoms, and support the patient throughout their care journey. (Relationship 953)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="HISTOLOGY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Histology is used to make treatment decisions. (Relationship 1931)</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT (Entity 36)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CANCER PATIENTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT refers to the broad range of medical care and interventions provided to cancer patients after diagnosis, encompassing surgery, radiation therapy, chemotherapy, targeted therapy, systemic therapy, endocrine therapy, stem cell or hematopoietic cell transplant, clinical trials, and supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The approach for cervical cancer may include surgery, radiation therapy, chemoradiation, systemic therapy, and clinical trials, depending on how far the cancer has spread.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="SIDE EFFECTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
