    <edge source="CANCER TREATMENT" target="MEDICAL HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Medical history helps determine which treatment is best</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER TREATMENT" target="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fertility preservation is an important consideration for individuals who are about to begin cancer treatment. It should be considered and planned before starting cancer treatment, as cancer therapies such as chemotherapy, radiation, or surgery may affect fertility. Therefore, fertility preservation should be addressed prior to the initiation of cancer treatment to allow for the best possible options and outcomes. In summary, both consideration and planning for fertility preservation should occur before starting cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER TREATMENT" target="PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Preventing pregnancy during treatment is important because cancer treatment can damage sperm and ovaries.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER TREATMENT" target="SUPPORT GROUPS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Support groups provide social support during the stressful time of cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER TREATMENT" target="CANCER DIAGNOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A cancer diagnosis leads to treatment planning and the delivery of cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient should obtain copies of imaging results and keep them organized.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="DIAGNOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging and diagnosis are closely linked in the detection and evaluation of cancer. Imaging tests are commonly used as part of the diagnostic workup for various types of cancer, helping clinicians identify, locate, and assess suspicious areas. In the specific case of basal cell skin cancer, imaging studies can aid in diagnosis in certain instances, though they are not always required. Overall, imaging plays a supportive but important role in the broader diagnostic process for cancer, complementing other diagnostic methods.</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">IMAGING and BIOPSY are complementary procedures in the management of glioma and other cancers, though the order in which they are used depends on the clinical context.

In cases where a biopsy has already confirmed disease progression, IMAGING is the next step to determine whether the cancer is locally progressive or has become metastatic. This helps guide further treatment by clarifying the extent and spread of the disease.

Conversely, when initial IMAGING findings suggest the presence of a glioma, a BIOPSY is then performed as the next step to confirm the diagnosis. In this scenario, imaging serves as the screening or detection tool that prompts tissue sampling for pathological confirmation.

Thus, the relationship between IMAGING and BIOPSY is bidirectional: imaging can lead to biopsy when a suspicious lesion is found, and biopsy can lead to imaging when progression is confirmed, each serving distinct roles in diagnosis and disease monitoring. Together, they form an integrated diagnostic and surveillance pathway for brain tumors and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
