    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="BLACK PEOPLE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Black people are identified as being at greater risk for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">BLACK PEOPLE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="PEOPLE WITH INHERITED GENETIC MUTATIONS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">People with certain inherited genetic mutations are at greater risk for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">PEOPLE WITH INHERITED GENETIC MUTATIONS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Prostate cancer is a disease that originates in the prostate gland. It can be categorized into different stages based on how far it has progressed. Early-stage prostate cancer is one such category or stage of prostate cancer. This stage is defined by the fact that the cancer has not spread beyond the prostate gland. In other words, early-stage prostate cancer remains localized within the prostate, with no evidence of invasion into surrounding tissues or distant metastasis. As a subset of prostate cancer, early-stage prostate cancer represents a less advanced form of the disease, often associated with a better prognosis and more treatment options than later stages. Thus, while prostate cancer encompasses all stages of the disease, early-stage prostate cancer specifically refers to the confined, localized phase.</data>
      <data key="d8">EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Health care professionals describe prostate cancer aggressiveness, recommend screening, and treat the disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SMOKEFREE.GOV" target="CDC" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">SmokeFree.gov and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) are both recognized as online support resources for individuals seeking to quit tobacco use. Specifically, SmokeFree.gov is listed as an online support option to help people quit smoking or vaping, while the CDCs website, CDC.gov/tobacco, is also listed as an online support resource for quitting tobacco. Together, they serve as accessible digital tools for tobacco cessation, offering guidance and assistance to those looking to stop using tobacco products.</data>
      <data key="d8">CDC</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SMOKEFREE.GOV" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">SmokeFree.gov is an online support resource for the patient to quit smoking or vaping</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SMOKEFREE.GOV" target="SMOKING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">SmokeFree.gov is designed for people who smoke and want support quitting</data>
      <data key="d8">SMOKING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SMOKEFREE.GOV" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stopping smoking or vaping is essential to maximize the chance of cure and minimize side effects from cancer treatment; SmokeFree.gov is an online resource to help quit.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BETOBACCOFREE.GOV" target="CDC" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">BeTobaccoFree.gov and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) are both recognized as online support resources for individuals seeking to quit tobacco use. Specifically, the CDC provides these resources through its dedicated website, CDC.gov/tobacco. Together, BeTobaccoFree.gov and the CDC serve as accessible online support options to help people quit smoking or vaping. Both entities are listed as credible sources of assistance for tobacco cessation, offering guidance and resources for those looking to stop using tobacco products.</data>
      <data key="d8">CDC</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BETOBACCOFREE.GOV" target="SMOKING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">BeTobaccoFree.gov is a tobacco cessation resource related to smoking</data>
      <data key="d8">SMOKING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BETOBACCOFREE.GOV" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">BeTobaccoFree.gov is an online resource to help quit tobacco use, which improves cancer treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CDC.GOV/TOBACCO" target="CDC" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CDC.gov/tobacco is the official tobacco resource page of the CDC (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention). As a dedicated web resource maintained by the CDC, it is designed to provide reliable information and online support for individuals looking to quit smoking or vaping. The CDC is the organization responsible for and associated with this website, ensuring that the content and resources offered align with public health guidelines and evidence-based cessation strategies.</data>
      <data key="d8">CDC</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CDC.GOV/TOBACCO" target="SMOKING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CDC.gov/tobacco provides information about smoking and tobacco use</data>
      <data key="d8">SMOKING</data>
    </edge>
