    <edge source="PROGRESSION" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">For patients whose cancer shows progression either during or after chemotherapy, immunotherapy is recommended as the next line of treatment for those who are eligible. This approach applies when the disease advances despite or following completion of chemotherapy, establishing immunotherapy as the standard subsequent therapeutic option.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ECHOCARDIOGRAM" target="ULTRASOUND" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">An echocardiogram is a type of ultrasound. Specifically, an echocardiogram is an ultrasound procedure that uses sound waves to create pictures of the heart. In this context, ultrasound refers both to the broader imaging modality and to the technology that employs high-frequency sound waves to visualize internal structures. The echocardiogram, as a specialized application of ultrasound, focuses on the heart, allowing healthcare providers to examine its structure and function in detail. Together, these descriptions establish that the echocardiogram is a subset of ultrasound, distinguished by its use of sound waves to generate images of the heart.</data>
      <data key="d8">ECHOCARDIOGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ECHOCARDIOGRAM" target="DECREASED HEART FUNCTION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">An echocardiogram can show decreased heart function if the ejection fraction is lower than normal</data>
      <data key="d8">ECHOCARDIOGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEART TESTS" target="ECHOCARDIOGRAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">An echocardiogram is a type of heart test that uses ultrasound, or sound waves, to create pictures of the heart. This non-invasive procedure allows healthcare providers to evaluate the structure and function of the heart, providing detailed information about its chambers, valves, and overall performance. As one of the key heart tests, the echocardiogram helps in diagnosing and monitoring various cardiac conditions by visually assessing how well the heart pumps blood and how its components are working together.</data>
      <data key="d8">ECHOCARDIOGRAM</data>
    </edge>
