      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">EBRT is a standard treatment option for cervical cancer that aims radiation at the cancer site and nearby lymph nodes, often given in fractions over 5 to 6 weeks.</data>
      <data key="d8">EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY" target="RECTAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">For rectal cancer, external beam radiation therapy uses radiation to treat the tumor, but it can also damage the ovaries and reduce hormones needed for natural pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d8">EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY" target="BLADDER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">For bladder cancer, EBRT is the type of radiation therapy usually used; a large machine aims radiation at the tumor area.</data>
      <data key="d8">EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY" target="BREAST CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">For breast cancer, EBRT uses a machine called a linear accelerator to aim radiation at the whole breast and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d8">EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY" target="SIDE EFFECTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">EBRT causes common side effects during treatment.</data>
