    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="LYNCH SYNDROME" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Relation type: is associated with Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the diseases onset.</data>
      <data key="d8">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Relation type: is associated with Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited syndrome closely related to colon cancer. In classic FAP, the polyps characteristic of the condition can become invasive colon cancer, often by age 50. Thus, colon cancer is a major complication of this inherited syndrome, with FAP serving as a direct precursor condition.</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="COLORECTAL CANCER SCREENING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
